The virt board gets a high PCIe window on a host with a small IPA.
Two limits dropped it on an Apple M1 under HVF, whose guests have 36
bits (64 GiB): the high regions never start below 256 GiB (the legacy
layout), and the window is 512 GiB aligned to its size (40 bits). The
256 GiB floor now applies only where the PA space reaches it (else the
high regions follow the memory, as they do past 255 GiB of RAM), and
highmem-mmio-size may be set below its default, down to 256 MiB. A host
with 40 bits or more keeps the layout it had. 2ksbox's Windows 11 on Arm
machines ask for 16 GiB, for d3dpt-vga's 512 MiB frame buffer, which the
32-bit window (0x10000000-0x3eff0000) cannot hold aligned to its size.
Drop when upstream places the high regions for a small IPA.

diff --git a/hw/arm/virt.c b/hw/arm/virt.c
index 219597cabd..b1ad2e0516 100644
--- a/hw/arm/virt.c
+++ b/hw/arm/virt.c
@@ -2574,7 +2574,13 @@ static void virt_set_memmap(VirtMachineState *vms, int pa_bits)
         error_report("maxmem/slots too huge");
         exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
     }
-    if (base < vms->memmap[VIRT_MEM].base + LEGACY_RAMLIMIT_BYTES) {
+    /*
+     * 2ksbox: the legacy floor only where the PA space reaches it. Below
+     * that (an Apple M1 under HVF: 36 bits) every high region would fall
+     * off the top, the PCIe window with them, so they follow the memory.
+     */
+    if (base < vms->memmap[VIRT_MEM].base + LEGACY_RAMLIMIT_BYTES &&
+        vms->memmap[VIRT_MEM].base + LEGACY_RAMLIMIT_BYTES < BIT_ULL(pa_bits)) {
         base = vms->memmap[VIRT_MEM].base + LEGACY_RAMLIMIT_BYTES;
     }
 
@@ -3391,11 +3397,14 @@ static void virt_set_highmem_mmio_size(Object *obj, Visitor *v,
         return;
     }
 
-    if (size < DEFAULT_HIGH_PCIE_MMIO_SIZE) {
-        char *sz = size_to_str(DEFAULT_HIGH_PCIE_MMIO_SIZE);
-        error_setg(errp, "highmem-mmio-size cannot be set to a lower value "
-                         "than the default (%s)", sz);
-        g_free(sz);
+    /*
+     * 2ksbox: a smaller window is allowed too. The default 512 GiB one,
+     * aligned to its size, needs a 40-bit IPA, and an Apple M1 under HVF
+     * has 36 bits, so there the window is dropped and a 64-bit BAR has
+     * nowhere to go above 4 GiB.
+     */
+    if (size < 256 * MiB) {
+        error_setg(errp, "highmem-mmio-size cannot be below 256 MiB");
         return;
     }
 
